    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Built from 1122 to 1150 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii">Suryavarman II</a>'s state temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, Angkor Wat was gradually turned into a Buddhist site and, unlike most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> temples, was never completely abandoned.</li><li>Its plan is commonly read as Mount Meru, home of the gods: the five central towers stand for the mountain's peaks, the walls and moat for the ranges and ocean around it.</li><li>Restoration begun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecole-francaise-dextreme-orient">École française d'Extrême-Orient</a> in 1908 was broken off in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> era and has been coordinated internationally under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> since 1993.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Fourteen 17th-century inscriptions found around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> tell of Japanese Buddhist pilgrims settling beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> locals; one, Ukondayu Kazufusa, celebrated the Khmer New Year at Angkor Wat in 1632.</p></aside>
    </section>

